    "title": "Scaling Compute Instead of Parameters: Exploring Iterative Transformers for Data-Efficient Language Modeling",
    "citation": "Wiktor Kamzela et al., 2026",
    "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/79e57f18c20b345f2d120f367ce9fb9b85d24123",
    "authors": "Wiktor Kamzela; Ondˇrej Dušek; Mateusz Lango",
    "year": "2026",
    "original_source": false
  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study compares held-out masked-language-model perplexity across iterative architectures that keep trainable parameter counts fixed while changing iteration count. This bears on whether architecture and effective depth alter loss at a fixed parameter count, but it does not fit or test scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites scaling laws as background but does not fit power laws, vary training tokens or compute systematically, or test extrapolation or compute-optimal allocation. It reports downstream benchmark scores, but does not test whether those scores are predictable from scale or pretraining loss.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites Kaplan and Hoffmann only as motivation. It fits no power law and does not vary tokens or compute systematically. Its main manipulation, looping shared layers at fixed trainable parameters, raises per-token compute and effective depth rather than changing shape at a fixed parameter count as V4 means. The setting is data-bound (10M tokens, 10 epochs, best validation checkpoint, one seed), so none of V1–V7 is tested.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that reusing a few layers through iteration is an effective alternative to scaling parameters on 10M-token BabyLM. A 3-layer iterated model (notably Middle-loop Repetition) beats a 24-layer baseline on SuperGLUE and a 6-layer baseline on BLiMP. LoRA specialization and embedding compression cut parameters while often improving performance. They draw no scaling-law conclusions.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study tests iterative, layer-sharing architectures in a data-constrained language-modeling setup; it does not reproduce the scaling-law measurements or fitting procedures of the cited original sources.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Perplexity differs across layer counts and iteration counts; the largest reported change is for L=1, N=16.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "At fixed trainable parameters, looping layers (more compute per token) lowers MLM perplexity for small L (L=1: 17.69 to 9.91 at N=8; L=3: 10.27 to 8.95 at N=16). For L=12 and L=24 the effect is small or perplexity worsens, and L=1 at N=16 degrades to 28.56.",
        "proposed_claim": "In a data-constrained regime, extra compute via weight-shared layer iteration at fixed trainable parameters reduces loss for shallow models, with diminishing or negative returns at larger depth or iteration counts.",
